/* Pass 16 — Authored story expansion. No mission graph changes, no invented consent.
   Each of the 188 mission openings has a unique additional physical/action beat.
   53 optional in-world dialogues are saveable and some produce later callbacks.
   Twelve endings receive additional world-specific closure. */
'use strict';
(function(g){
const textures={"A0-01":"Outside, rain taps the wrong side of the shop window. Inside, the brass bell hums as though someone has struck it and is still waiting for an answer.","HUB-00":"Pip climbs the reading table by way of a stack of dictionaries. He insists this is faster than the stairs, though the dictionaries disagree.","A0-02":"The final note is lower than the others. You feel it through the soles of your shoes, as if the building itself were trying to speak.","A0-03":"Pip holds the candle sideways. The silver marks throw long shadows, and neither shadow resembles a shoe or an animal's paw.","A0-04":"A little tack bends in Pip's hand. He drops the accusation strip and looks at the empty space where a fact ought to be.","A0-05":"The three keepsakes lie on blue velvet. None of them shines more brightly than the others; the choice is yours, not the cabinet's.","A0-06":"The map smells of old paper and a little sea salt. Pip swears that last night it smelled of cinnamon instead.","A0-07":"At the crossing, three lamps glow in different colors. You hear rain, high wind, and ballroom music coming from roads only a few steps apart.","A1-W01":"Red keeps checking the little patch of darkness behind every lantern. She walks quickly, but never so quickly that you have to run to follow.","A1-W02":"Three different kinds of wet earth cling to your boots. The rivers meet ahead, but the tracks beside them tell three different stories.","A1-W03":"The Wolf stays behind the gate even after you step closer. His ears turn toward every footfall, yet he gives you room to decide whether to speak.","A1-W04":"When you crouch, the ink shines violet beneath the daylight. A real pawprint would have pressed the mud aside; this one sits on top like paint.","A1-W05":"Grandmother pours tea into a cup with a chipped handle. She leaves one chair empty and keeps glancing at it between sentences.","A1-W06":"The ribbon is worn thin where many fingers have held it. Red smooths its edge and waits for Grandmother to decide what she is willing to share.","A1-W07":"A small lamp burns inside the root house, hidden beneath a clay pot. Someone has chosen a careful signal instead of calling for help aloud.","A1-W08":"The courier's hands tremble when the word testimony is spoken. Outside, an ordinary bird hops along the sill, utterly unaware of the choice inside.","A1-W09":"A howl rolls through the valley twice, exactly the same each time. Red stops walking. Natural voices do not usually repeat like a clockwork toy.","A1-W10":"At the village bridge, the lamps are lit again. Their warm circles are small, but people have begun stepping into them without looking over their shoulders.","HUB-01":"Quill sets out a fresh cup for every traveler, whether they brought home answers or only better questions. Pip has already labeled each cup with a tiny paper name.","A1-C01":"Wind catches Jack's hair and flings it across his eyes. He laughs once, then grows serious when he sees the families waiting at the broken crossing.","A1-C02":"The ledger's pages flutter without wind. Between two dates there is a clean white strip where the paper has not been torn at all.","A1-C03":"Bram's pencil is longer than your arm, but the numbers in his calendar are neat and small. He uses the eraser with extraordinary care.","A1-C04":"Inside the clock housing, one hand jerks backward while the others move on. The click is faint enough to disappear beneath Jack's nervous whistling.","A1-C05":"The seed floats a finger's width above your open palm. Far below, the bridge ropes pull tight as a gust strikes the valley.","A1-C06":"The Crow has arranged his feathers to look official. His lost envelope, however, still carries a flour mark from a bakery nowhere near his usual route.","A1-C07":"The weather bridge disappears into cloud halfway across. Families watch from both ends, waiting for someone to offer a plan they can actually trust.","A1-C08":"Jack scratches the back of his neck, then stops. He sets down his climbing rope as carefully as if it could hear the truth he is about to tell.","A1-C09":"Bram spreads the calendar beneath a window bright with cloudlight. Every date has its own ink blot; the missing day has none.","A1-C10":"A village bell rings from the far side of the bridge. People answer it with little hand-held bells, making a noisy promise to watch out for one another.","A1-P01":"The waltz begins for the third time, and a violinist almost drops her bow. Cinderella notices at once; she is counting more than dance steps.","A1-P02":"The empty chair has a folded napkin and a warm glass. Someone prepared a place here, even if the official list says no guest was expected.","A1-P03":"In the mirror, the chandeliers gleam brighter than they do in the room. Cinderella stands beside you but keeps her eyes on the real floor.","A1-P04":"The cabinet smells of cedar and sealing wax. A thin line of dust tells you that the top drawer has been opened recently, but not by whom.","A1-P05":"The false sentence sits awkwardly between the original lines. Its letters crowd one another as though somebody was afraid of running out of room.","A1-P06":"The clock approaches twelve, and the servants exchange glances. One older footman counts each beat on his thumb instead of dancing.","A1-P07":"Cinderella folds her hands to keep them still. She wants to tell the truth about her own choice without exposing the guest she was trying to protect.","A1-P08":"Downstairs, the servants know the sound of each bell better than anyone at the ball. Their corridor smells of lemon soap and cooling bread.","A1-P09":"The herald's words catch in his throat. A crowded ballroom can be loud, but the silence waiting for his next sentence is louder.","A1-P10":"The ledger closes with a firm, soft thump. Cinderella breathes out, though she knows the larger question—who ordered the lie—still remains.","A2-01":"Across the reading table, three kingdom maps overlap without covering one another. Pip lays his paw on the only blank place they share.","A2-02":"The extra stitch pulls at the map even when nobody touches it. Pip's ears turn pink; for once he has no clever remark ready.","A2-03":"The letter's seal is whole, but the delivery box is empty. Someone carried this message a long way and did not want its name written down.","A2-04":"Pip leaves the missing-name space open on the board. He has drawn a little lantern beside it, small enough to fit under one thumbnail.","A2-05":"The Wolf listens from the edge of the room. He does not step closer simply because you have finally found better evidence.","A2-06":"The second kingdom appears on the map as a patch of moving light. Quill slides a clean page beneath it so the ink cannot blur.","A2-07":"Grandmother opens her notebook at a page stiff with pressed leaves. The writing is narrow, but the dates have not changed.","A2-08":"The station bell rings, and the platform shivers beneath the Cloudreach wind. A clerk steadies the record with both hands.","A2-09":"Nell meets you beneath a corridor lamp that smells faintly of hot metal. She has left the crowded ballroom on purpose.","REC-01":"Grandmother turns the original toward you but keeps one palm on the page. You can study it without carrying away the only copy.","A2-10":"At Crossing Three, the lantern housings hang empty, their hooks creaking. You can still see scratches where a careful keeper once checked each hinge.","REC-02":"The station audit book has a tear shaped like a narrow window. Behind the gap, the next page still carries the pressure of a missing line.","REC-03":"A servant spreads the tally beside a plate of cold cakes. She checks the numbers twice before deciding whether her name should appear with them.","A2-11":"The Keeper's appointment survives on ordinary paper, not a glowing spell. Its corners are worn from years of being unfolded for travelers.","A2-12":"The road ahead smells of sugar long before any cottage comes into view. Pip hopes this means lunch; Quill advises caution.","HUB-02":"A new shelf has appeared overnight, narrow enough for one book. Pip measures it three times and declares that shelves ought not grow while anyone is sleeping.","A2-X01":"The old court record opens to a date you remember differently. The memory is useful, but the paper in front of you belongs to this telling.","A2-X02":"Nell studies you with polite puzzlement. She remembers a different visitor, and it would be unfair to ask her to trust a memory she does not share.","A2-X03":"The fresh count is written on a scrap of brown wrapping paper. Its modest appearance does not make it any less important.","A2-X04":"Pip pins two contradictory statements side by side. He draws no connecting arrow until someone shows him a reason.","A3-01":"The bridge rails are dusted with sugar, yet somebody has nailed a very ordinary warning sign across the path. The warning is not part of any recipe.","REC-09":"Under the candy-colored paint, the bridge has real bolts and rotten wood. Hansel's tools are cheerful; the repair work is not a game.","A3-02":"Hansel has seven unfinished inventions on one table and insists that all seven are working beautifully. One releases a puff of cinnamon when you touch it.","A3-03":"Gretel's calendar smells like apples and ink. Her recipe dates are neat enough that a changed number stands out immediately.","A3-04":"Taffy Thorn folds the corner of a receipt until it nearly tears. She is angry, but she keeps checking that nobody in her shop is listening.","A3-05":"The ingredient jars look identical from across the room. Up close, one paper label has been scraped and pasted over another.","A3-06":"The ferry rocks against its rope while passengers compare tickets. A second ledger lies in a waterproof tin beneath the seat.","REC-04":"Taffy unlocks the drawer herself. The old purchase book is sticky at the corners, with a faint thumbprint pressed into the last recorded sale.","A3-07":"The recipe looks harmless until you read the price. Gretel presses a finger against the sentence, keeping her voice level for Hansel's sake.","A3-08":"A drop of blue dye blooms in water beside the signature. The ink does not dissolve the way it should, and that difference deserves a careful note.","A3-09":"Hansel has already started moving the crates before you can offer advice. He is not waiting to be a sidekick in somebody else's story.","A3-10":"Gretel lays three dates in a row, then turns the wrong one face down. She wants you to test her reasoning, not simply agree with her.","A3-11":"A familiar stitch appears beside a candy-box seam. It looks like the bookshop mark, but the paper it holds is newer.","A3-12":"The Archive notice arrives under a ribbon that was tied too tightly. Somebody expects this document to matter far beyond the market.","HUB-03":"The view beyond the bookshop window is all gingerbread roofs for a heartbeat, then ordinary dark sky again. Pip smells the glass to see if the sugar followed.","A4-01":"The Royal Archive has three doors, and every one shows a different kind of wear. People have been using all of them, whether the rules admit it or not.","A4-02":"The last sentence ends in a neat cut. The missing space is so clean that you can imagine somebody holding the page still while they worked.","A4-03":"Two catalogs rest on separate stands. One smells of damp paper, the other of fresh linen, and their records were kept by different hands.","A4-04":"The assistant keeps both palms flat on the table. The room is full of old mistakes, but this person should not have to carry them all.","A4-05":"Archivist Tern keeps his glasses in a velvet case with a broken clasp. He pauses before opening the order, as if the sound might be heard down the hall.","REC-05":"The recorded testimony desk is tucked behind a curtain. Two chairs face the same window instead of one another, giving the witness an easier place to look.","A4-06":"The copied order has the same crooked line in both versions. The imperfection is revealing: two sheets may have come from the same damaged original.","A4-07":"In the restoration room, warm paste smells like flour. Quill runs a finger just above the repaired seam without touching the fragile page.","A4-08":"Nella's file fits in a narrow drawer beneath the counter. Dust covers the drawer handle but not the keyhole.","A4-09":"An empty name box has been crossed through with ruler-straight ink. The surrounding handwriting is much less certain.","A4-10":"A long table separates the sources into little stacks. Pip looks relieved to have something he can count, even if he cannot decide what the stacks mean alone.","A4-11":"The rescued page crackles when the wrapping comes away. Its blank edge is not empty; someone has carefully protected room for another ending.","A4-12":"Witnesses enter the hall without crowns or official badges. Their words must be weighed by what they know, not by the height of their chairs.","A4-13":"The doors to the court open slowly. Behind them, dozens of people are quietly discussing endings that were never supposed to be missing.","HUB-04":"Quill lights the lamp in the restoration room herself. Pip, usually eager to chatter, brings a second chair and says nothing.","A5-01":"The royal court smells of polished wood and rain-damp cloaks. Someone has set out water for every witness, including those who may say nothing.","A5-02":"An empty chair stands beside the Wolf's name. Whether he takes it must be his decision; the chair itself proves nothing.","A5-03":"A wax impression catches the candlelight. Its crown looks correct until the tiny chipped star on the rim gives it away.","REC-11":"The seal-maker's tools lie in precise order. She refuses to call two matching copies independent without checking where each one began.","A5-04":"Jack looks smaller without his climbing rope. He waits through an uncomfortable silence before admitting what he did and what he did not do.","A5-05":"The ballroom evidence reaches the court wrapped in plain cloth. Nell carries the count herself and has crossed out every detail she never agreed to share.","A5-06":"The accusation board stands in full view of the gallery. A name written here will travel farther than anything whispered in a corridor.","A5-07":"A correction is harder to say than a suspicion. The clerk holds his pen above the paper until you choose words you can stand behind.","REC-08":"The public notice has space for only a few lines. That makes it more important to name exactly which accusation has been withdrawn.","A5-08":"The crown seal lies beside an order it never approved. The gap between authority and appearance is small enough to fit under a fingertip.","A5-09":"A black feather lands on the rail, then runs like a drop of ink. The watching crowd grows still, waiting to see what the creature will decide.","A5-10":"Two accounts remain on the board after the false one is removed. Both tell part of the truth, and the differences are still worth preserving.","A5-11":"The court's final words are read slowly enough for the witnesses to hear. Not every unanswered question is a failure of justice.","A5-12":"Outside the court, one distant bell begins to ring. The trial can correct a public wrong, but something beyond the city is still working.","A5-13":"Pip rolls the court papers into a tube and nearly disappears inside it. Nobody laughs until he emerges wearing the tube like an enormous hat.","HUB-05":"The bookshop is busy with people who have nowhere else to discuss the trial. Quill leaves the door open and lets them choose whether to speak.","A6-01":"At the Margin Gate, the horizon bends like a folded sheet. The ground under your shoes feels real even when the road drawn across it does not.","A6-02":"Someone has built a vegetable garden at the edge of a blank map. The carrots grow normally, which makes the missing road stranger, not less real.","A6-03":"A fox's footprint ends beneath a drifting black feather. A nearby child points out that both arrived at precisely the same moment.","A6-04":"The unsent instruction has been folded and unfolded until its creases are white. Its words describe a job; they cannot describe every choice the messenger later made.","A6-05":"A survivor holds the stolen page against their chest. You cannot read its ending unless they move their hand, and they are entitled to keep it there.","A6-06":"Rumpelstiltskin's spinning wheel clicks pleasantly while the contract lies open. The sound becomes less pleasant after you read the small print.","REC-07":"The witness has chosen a quiet bench near running water. They asked for a place where leaving would be easy.","A6-07":"The Margin King's hall contains no mirrors. The walls are lined with drawings of old dangers, each one labeled with a solution that leaves no room for anyone to disagree.","A6-08":"The directive looks tidy. Everything has a box, a category, a safe answer. Even the spaces for names have been made too small.","A6-09":"A bead of ink stops at the edge of a page that could still be harmed. The thief waits long enough for you to notice the hesitation.","A6-11":"At Mirror Mere, the water shows a road that is not reflected in the sky. A soft reed brushes your wrist as if asking you to look again.","A6-Y01":"A scrap of the thief's first assignment smells faintly of cold ash. Its oldest line was written before several later orders were copied.","A6-Y02":"The unburned page is folded beneath a stone. The charred edges stop at the words someone chose not to destroy.","A6-Y03":"The workshop walls are covered in sketches of a creature changing shape. None of the drawings can answer what it should become next.","A6-10":"The sorter ticks, the binder pulls, and the road-cutter hums. Three sounds, three kinds of harm, and three separate problems to stop.","A6-12":"Nella's door stands where no door should fit. A paper flower rests on the latch, its petals worn soft from being handled.","A6-X01":"The survivor holds two possible futures on separate cards. Neither card is yours to choose without asking the person who must live it.","A6-X02":"The council seats have been placed in a circle. Nobody sits at the head, because there is no head of a circle.","A6-X03":"The missing lever has left a bright patch on the machine frame. A hand once reached here, and someone made sure no one could reach again.","A6-13":"The return road has the smell of rain even though no clouds are overhead. The bookshop bell is the first familiar sound you hear.","HUB-06":"Quill makes space among the half-mended books. Every rescued page is placed where its owner can find it and refuse a repair.","A7-01":"The bell diagram has been repaired with different colors of thread. Someone wanted future readers to see where the old drawing had been damaged.","REC-06":"The apprentice's sketch includes a smudged thumbprint and a note about an ordinary hammer. Important work was done by real hands, not an all-powerful bell.","A7-02":"The witness chooses their own chair at the meeting. Pip leaves another chair empty in case they want more space.","A7-03":"The coalition table is crowded with maps, snacks, and disagreement. Red pulls a chair closer to the edge so the smallest people can see.","A7-04":"Bram's careful diagram leaves room for people on both sides of the bridge. Jack has sketched a daring shortcut in the margin, then crossed it out.","A7-05":"Cinderella brings a stack of guest lists with names covered. The servants bring the more important question: who gets to make the rule?","A7-06":"The Wolf studies the proposed witness stand. He knows what a crowd can do with a story it wants to believe.","REC-10":"The consent circle has no raised seat and no locked door. Each person can say yes to one part, no to another, or leave.","A7-07":"Pip balances three small brass gears on a saucer. They spin together until he removes one, and the saucer falls silent.","A7-X01":"The survivors draw their own border with chalk, then rub out part of it so that a guest may leave freely.","A7-X02":"Three anchors lie on the table, each entrusted to a different keeper. None will work alone, and no one is required to surrender control of their own.","A7-X03":"The agreement leaves a space for dissenting names. A person who disagrees has not disappeared from the room.","A7-08":"The plan board is full of arrows, crossed-out risks, and borrowed ink. Quill asks every volunteer to point at one part they still do not understand.","REC-12":"A supply cart rolls in with two bad wheels and plenty of good intentions. Repairing it is less glorious than magic, and much more useful.","A7-09":"The caravan creaks under spare lantern glass, rope, and carefully labeled records. The packers check the list again before anyone leaves.","A7-10":"At the road's edge, you can hear the bookshop bell very faintly. Nobody offers you a prophecy; your next steps are still yours.","HUB-07":"Pip has put out extra blankets. He pretends they are for the books, though each one is folded beside a chair someone might use tonight.","A8-01":"The Broken Chime Tower rises above a road of loose stone. Every step shakes grit from the walls, and each companion watches a different danger.","A8-X01":"Beneath the tower, survivor-made markers point to a safe passage. They are small enough to miss unless you slow down and look.","A8-X02":"The emergency agreement has a stop signal in plain view. Anyone who asks to halt must be heard, even at the worst possible moment.","A8-X03":"The thirteenth path flickers behind a curtain of loose paper. Its owners stand together and keep their hands on the latch.","A8-02":"The public witness lantern casts light across the machine without exposing the private names sealed inside its record box.","A8-03":"Three heavy levers groan. Pulling one without securing the others could turn a rescue into another stolen future.","A8-04":"The master seal lies between you and the King. It would be easy to pick it up and make decisions for everyone. That is precisely what makes it dangerous.","A8-05":"Twelve plans sit beside twelve different responsibilities. None is a prize awarded for finding the correct button.","A8-06":"The bellkeeper plan is laid out on a table beside a common hammer. Its strength will come from the people trusted to ring the warning.","END-E01":"The warning bell is carried through the square by ordinary neighbors. It belongs to no throne and sounds only when people need to know.","A8-07":"The single page rests in a soft cloth. Its owner has read the proposed words and keeps a hand on the margin where a change may be refused.","END-E02":"The restored line settles into the page as gently as a bookmark. Nobody cheers until its owner smiles and says it may stay.","A8-08":"Three messengers arrive with different kinds of help. The plans are not identical, but the people who made them want the same roads to remain open.","END-E03":"The kingdoms celebrate in their own ways. Cloudreach rings copper bells, the forest lights lanterns, and the palace opens its doors to the servants first.","A8-09":"Two road markers are planted side by side. People can read both, but the decision to travel belongs to the person standing at the fork.","END-E04":"The new map shows more than one road home. No ruler draws a line through the alternatives to keep the page neat.","A8-10":"The original directive lies open under a lamp. Every copied claim is traced back to its source before a name is spoken aloud.","END-E05":"When the public record is corrected, the clerk leaves the old mistake visible with a clear mark beside it: changed because new evidence was found.","A8-11":"The Wolf stands beside the evidence rather than beneath a royal banner. He wants the record corrected, but he does not owe the crowd a performance.","END-E06":"Children cross the village path while the Wolf watches from the shade. The new warning signs tell travelers what to look for, not whom to fear by reputation.","A8-12":"Bram spreads the calendar across two broad tables. Jack holds one corner still and waits for the Giant to finish explaining the dates.","END-E07":"In Cloudreach, an ordinary market day arrives in its proper place. Bakers open their stalls, and Bram writes the date slowly enough to enjoy it.","A8-13":"The palace clock waits behind a curtain. Cinderella asks the servants to speak first about what a fair midnight should mean.","END-E08":"At midnight, the music ends because the guests and servants choose to stop. Nobody has to wait for permission from a forged rule.","A8-14":"The Ink Thief rests in the shape of a dark bird beside a list of harms it has agreed to repair. The list has no line promising automatic forgiveness.","END-E09":"A small black feather marks the first completed repair. The people harmed decide for themselves whether the apology is enough.","A8-15":"Copies of the true record travel to places no crown can reach in one command. Each carries the name of its keeper and the date it was made.","END-E10":"The royal record becomes a collection of local records, all able to correct one another when someone makes a mistake.","A8-16":"The empty throne is pushed to the back of the hall. In its place stands a long table, easier to leave and harder to rule from.","END-E11":"No single voice announces the future. The new caretakers disagree in public, write down their decisions, and remain answerable to the people affected.","A8-17":"The survivor council checks every boundary one final time. The extraordinary path is still allowed to close whenever its people wish.","END-E12":"The thirteenth road glows briefly, then dims to an ordinary safe path. Its owners keep the key, and the others learn to ask before entering.","A8-18":"The three-part machine is silent. A careful inspector confirms each part separately before anyone calls the danger over.","A8-19":"Far to the north, an answer chime reaches the bookshop like a note from another page. It does not undo tonight's victory.","HUB-08":"Pip slides the new story into the Living Atlas without closing its neighboring pages. Your choices have a home, and another road waits for another telling.","A1-R01":"You recognize the shape of the chime, but the window is different this time. Familiarity gives you a question, not a shortcut to the answer.","A1-R02":"Mirror Mere shivers with blue light. Eira watches the reeds instead of the reflections, searching for a mark water cannot imitate.","A1-R03":"A place in the reflected crowd remains empty while the real shore fills with people. You count again before drawing a conclusion.","A1-R04":"The old order was written in a hand from another time. Its date matters more than its resemblance to later instructions.","A1-R05":"Eira steps aside so you can compare the two surfaces without her shadow. She will help investigate, but not be judged by an image.","A1-R06":"Aster's foot stops over a patch of wet earth. The interrupted print tells of a sudden change of direction, not who caused it.","A1-R07":"The two owners have drawn their boundaries in different ink. Neither signature permits a traveler to use the whole road without asking.","A1-R08":"The mirror shows one arrival; the toll notebook shows another. Both records deserve attention, and neither can simply swallow the other.","A1-R09":"A witness agrees to a date but not a name. Pip leaves the name field blank instead of trying to guess.","A1-R10":"The fourth lantern burns with a small steady flame. You cannot name this road for its owners, but you can remember that it exists."};
const dialogues={"A0-01":{"speaker":"Pip","setup":"Pip tips his tiny hat and asks whether you think a shop can invite a visitor. His whiskers twitch with excitement. “I always hope the answer is yes. It would be rude for a place to summon you and then not offer a chair.”","label0":"Ask what he would do first.","reply0":"“Look for something ordinary that is behaving oddly,” Pip says. “An extraordinary thing acting extraordinary is practically showing off.” He taps the backward sign. “You can borrow my pencil,” Pip adds. “It is almost as tall as you. That is its best quality.”","label1":"Ask whether he is ever frightened.","reply1":"Pip grips his pencil. “Quite often. But I take notes so the fear doesn't get to be the only thing talking.” “I still get scared,” Pip adds. “I just refuse to let fear hold the pencil.”"},"HUB-00":{"speaker":"Mistress Quill","setup":"Quill sets down a cup, though she hasn't asked whether you want tea. “When a story frightens you,” she says, “you can stop and look again. You never owe a book your courage all at once.”","label0":"Tell her you want to be brave.","reply0":"“Then start by being kind to yourself when you're unsure,” Quill says. “Bravery and certainty are different things.” Quill moves your cup closer. “Even brave people need a place to put down their boots.”","label1":"Tell her you want to understand everything.","reply1":"“You won't,” Quill replies with a small smile. “Neither will I. Let's promise not to pretend.” “Start with one question,” Quill says. “We have an entire shop for the others.”"},"A0-05":{"speaker":"Pip","setup":"Pip inspects the old key, ribbon, and compass. “I once chose a silver spoon as my adventuring tool,” he confesses. “Very useful for pudding. Slightly less useful for locked doors.”","label0":"Ask which keepsake he would take.","reply0":"“Whichever reminds me why I came,” Pip answers. “A tool is better when you understand what you mean to do with it.” Pip pats his waistcoat. “I also recommend pockets. Preferably more than two.”","label1":"Ask whether a wrong keepsake can ruin the quest.","reply1":"“Not in Quill's shop,” Pip says fiercely. “No one gets a bad ending for choosing a ribbon instead of a key.” He checks his pockets. “I have survived every one of my own silly choices so far.”"},"A0-07":{"speaker":"Mistress Quill","setup":"Quill listens to all three roads. “You'll meet people who look like the characters you know,” she says. “Remember that a famous story is only part of a person's life.”","label0":"Promise to listen before judging.","reply0":"Quill nods. “You may be the first traveler this week who has promised that.” “And try to ask twice when the first answer sounds too easy,” Quill adds.","label1":"Ask whether all three roads are dangerous.","reply1":"“All three need help,” she answers. “Danger and the chance to do good are not the same thing.” “You may feel afraid,” Quill says. “That does not mean you chose the wrong road.”"},"A1-W01":{"speaker":"Red Riding Hood","setup":"Red lifts the lantern to examine your face. “People keep asking why I still go into these woods,” she says. “They think one bad day should decide every other day.”","label0":"Ask what makes the woods worth visiting.","reply0":"“The people,” Red says. “And the mushrooms after rain. Especially the people, though the mushrooms are excellent.” Red lifts her lantern. “The best part is that the woods still surprise me.”","label1":"Ask whether she trusts the Wolf.","reply1":"“That's for him and me to work out,” Red says. “You can help by finding out what happened today.” Red tugs her hood straight. “I will listen to facts before rumors.”"},"A1-W03":{"speaker":"The Wolf","setup":"The Wolf speaks through the gate without stepping toward you. “I know how the story sounds,” he says. “A wolf. A missing messenger. A howl in the trees.” His voice is low but steady.","label0":"Ask what he wants you to examine.","reply0":"“My paws. The mud. The path. Things you can check without trusting my word,” he answers. The Wolf steps aside so you can see the stone clearly. He does not demand thanks.","label1":"Ask if the accusations make him angry.","reply1":"“They do. I can be angry and still tell the truth. I'd like the village to learn both things are possible.” “I would rather people knew the real me,” he says, “even the angry parts.”"},"A1-W05":{"speaker":"Grandmother","setup":"Grandmother warms her palms around a mug. “Age has taught me a troublesome lesson,” she says. “I can remember every stitch in this blanket and still forget which visitor came first.”","label0":"Ask her to separate memory from certainty.","reply0":"“A sensible request,” she says, taking down the date book. “I will tell you what I wrote, and separately what I remember.” Grandmother pushes the warm biscuit plate closer. “We can talk and eat. Both help.”","label1":"Ask how she feels about the missing courier.","reply1":"Her face softens. “Like somebody has left a candle burning in an empty room. I would like to know they're safe.” “Nobody gets every detail right at once,” Grandmother says. “Not even me.”"},"A1-W07":{"speaker":"The Lantern Courier","setup":"The courier keeps a hand near the little lamp. “Everyone wants the name,” they whisper. “No one asked if saying it could put my family in danger.”","label0":"Offer to step outside while they decide.","reply0":"The courier's shoulders settle. “Thank you. I can tell you where the lantern went. The rest is mine to decide.”","label1":"Ask what would help them feel safer.","reply1":"“A door I can leave by. A witness who doesn't shout. And the right to stop talking.”"},"A1-W10":{"speaker":"Red Riding Hood","setup":"Red looks down at the record and folds her cloak around her elbows. “I expected a simple villain,” she admits. “It would have been easier. It would also have been wrong.”","label0":"Tell her changing a theory took courage.","reply0":"Red smiles. “So does changing a habit. I think we all have some work to do.”","label1":"Ask whether she'll stay in the woods.","reply1":"“Of course. It's my home. A bad story doesn't get to chase me from it.”"},"HUB-01":{"speaker":"Pip","setup":"Pip has counted the pages you brought home twice. “The strange thing,” he says, “is how heavy a true account can feel even when it is written on one thin sheet.”","label0":"Ask if he ever makes mistakes in his notes.","reply0":"“Frequently,” he admits. “This is why my eraser is nearly as long as my pencil.”","label1":"Ask what happens to people who still disagree.","reply1":"“We keep their statements,” he says. “A story isn't fair merely because everybody is tired of arguing.”"},"A1-C01":{"speaker":"Jack","setup":"Jack balances on a fence rail and points toward the storm. “Last time I saw a bridge this bad, I tried jumping it. Didn't tell anyone first. Felt wonderful for about three seconds.”","label0":"Ask what happened after the jump.","reply0":"“The rope snapped. Nobody got hurt, but somebody might have. I don't want my best idea to be the one that scares everyone else.” Jack tightens his rope knot. “Next time I promise to check it before shouting ‘Adventure!’”","label1":"Tell him you like taking risks too.","reply1":"Jack grins. “Then we should learn which risks are ours to take and which ones other people must live with.” Jack looks at the ground below. “I am working on the looking-before-leaping part.”"},"A1-C03":{"speaker":"Bram","setup":"Bram bends so you don't have to shout. “A day looks small to somebody counting from a distance,” he says. “For the baker, it is bread that never rose. For the children, it's a birthday nobody remembers.”","label0":"Ask how he knows the date is wrong.","reply0":"“I count because people rely on my calendar,” Bram says. “And I keep the pages even when a ruler says they are inconvenient.” Bram taps his pencil. “Dates do not mind if we are excited. They still need to be right.”","label1":"Ask whether giants ever forget days.","reply1":"“All the time,” Bram admits. “That's why I write them down.” “A clock can be fixed,” Bram says. “A person needs a little more patience.”"},"A1-C07":{"speaker":"Jack","setup":"Jack tests a knot without looking up. “I can cross faster,” he says. “That's not what they're asking. They're asking whether their grandparents and little brothers can cross safely.”","label0":"Tell him helping others changes the plan.","reply0":"“Yes,” Jack says. “It's less impressive. I think it might be better.”","label1":"Ask him to make the decision alone.","reply1":"Jack shakes his head. “The people on the bridge deserve a say. I won't choose for them again.”"},"A1-C08":{"speaker":"Jack","setup":"“I told myself it was a harmless shortcut,” Jack says. He smooths the paper and doesn't quite meet your eye. “Then I saw what it did to Bram's dates.”","label0":"Ask what he'll do to repair the damage.","reply0":"“Fix the signs. Tell Bram. Stay when people are upset. I can't make the shortcut unhappen.”","label1":"Tell him admitting it must be difficult.","reply1":"“It is,” Jack says. “But difficulty isn't proof I'm doing something brave. I still have to put things right.”"},"A1-P01":{"speaker":"Cinderella","setup":"Cinderella stands just beyond the ballroom doors, one slipper resting on the stone. “People expect me to love every ball,” she says. “Tonight I would rather have a cup of tea and a normal clock.”","label0":"Ask what she noticed first.","reply0":"“The musicians looked frightened,” she answers. “They knew the song before it started again.” Cinderella glances toward the servants’ door. “The ballroom is not the whole palace.”","label1":"Ask if she wants to leave.","reply1":"“Not until the servants can leave too. Then I would love some tea.” “We can ask without demanding an answer,” Cinderella says."},"A1-P07":{"speaker":"Cinderella","setup":"Cinderella looks toward the guest gallery. “I changed one small thing to protect someone,” she says. “Now everyone wants to know whether I changed the rest.”","label0":"Ask her to explain only her own edit.","reply0":"“I can do that,” she says. “I won't name the guest without permission, even to save myself trouble.”","label1":"Tell her secrets always cause harm.","reply1":"“Sometimes,” she answers carefully. “And sometimes people need privacy. We must learn which is which.”"},"A1-P08":{"speaker":"Nell","setup":"Nell folds a clean towel across her wrist. “The people who polish the clock know when it skips,” she says. “We hear it at night after everyone else has gone home.”","label0":"Ask how to record her account safely.","reply0":"“Let me decide which parts have my name on them. I can give you the chair count either way.”","label1":"Ask if she blames Cinderella.","reply1":"“That's too easy an answer,” Nell says. “Show me what she changed, and what somebody else did.”"},"HUB-02":{"speaker":"Mistress Quill","setup":"Quill traces the empty line where the Lantern Keeper's name should be. “Absence can be a clue,” she says, “but we must not fill an empty line just because a story feels unfinished.”","label0":"Ask whether the Keeper might still be safe.","reply0":"“Yes. Or in danger. Our duty is to search without turning a possibility into a fact.”","label1":"Ask if Quill knows the Keeper personally.","reply1":"“I have known their work,” she replies. “And I owe them a more careful search than guessing.”"},"A2-02":{"speaker":"Pip","setup":"“My stitching is crooked on purpose,” Pip declares, then looks at the map and sighs. “This stitch isn't mine. It is much too neat.”","label0":"Ask if he feels responsible.","reply0":"“I repair maps,” he says. “That doesn't mean every mark on every map belongs to me. I should remember my own lesson.”","label1":"Tell him the new stitch might help.","reply1":"His ears lift. “Then let's measure it before it starts trying to become a mystery all by itself.”"},"A2-05":{"speaker":"The Wolf","setup":"The Wolf watches you correct the account. He doesn't smile. “You have done something necessary,” he says. “I don't know yet whether I want to travel beside you.”","label0":"Tell him he owes you nothing.","reply0":"He gives a small nod. “Then perhaps we can speak another day.”","label1":"Ask him to forgive you immediately.","reply1":"“Not yet,” he answers. “I can hear your apology without promising what I feel.”"},"A2-11":{"speaker":"Pip","setup":"Pip lays the Keeper's record beside the missing-name board. “Someone kept this paper,” he says. “I wish they could tell us why they were brave enough to keep it.”","label0":"Ask what he hopes to find next.","reply0":"“A safe road for the Keeper,” Pip replies. “And answers that will stand up if somebody asks how we know.”","label1":"Tell him this proves the Keeper is alive.","reply1":"“No,” Pip says softly. “It proves they were appointed. That's precious, but it isn't the same thing.”"},"A3-02":{"speaker":"Hansel","setup":"Hansel wipes a streak of flour off a small copper device. “It's a biscuit finder,” he announces. The device whistles at your pocket. “I may need to adjust what counts as a biscuit.”","label0":"Ask him to help with the ledger.","reply0":"“Of course! I'll use a ruler this time. Things go wrong when I measure dates with the biscuit finder.” Hansel smiles. “It only caught fire a little. That counts as progress in my workshop.”","label1":"Ask about Gretel's opinion.","reply1":"“She tests my ideas before I blow anything up,” Hansel says. “Which sounds unfair until you see what happened to the bread oven.” “Gretel says I should test things twice,” Hansel admits. “She is usually right.”"},"A3-03":{"speaker":"Gretel","setup":"Gretel closes her recipe book with one floury finger between the pages. “People believe me when I bring cake,” she says. “I wish they listened just as closely when I bring dates.”","label0":"Ask what matters about the recipe calendar.","reply0":"“I wrote the ingredients before the market changed the labels. A copied list made later can't replace that.” Gretel closes her recipe book. “A good recipe is like a good clue. The order matters.”","label1":"Tell her Hansel should solve it for you.","reply1":"Gretel raises an eyebrow. “He has useful ideas. So do I. You'll need both if we're going to fix this.” “I can be wrong,” Gretel says. “But I want to know exactly where.”"},"A3-04":{"speaker":"Taffy Thorn","setup":"Taffy looks at her shop window, where frightened customers have begun avoiding the colored sweets. “If someone forged my order, they put people's trust in my hands and then made my hands look dirty.”","label0":"Ask to see her original ledger.","reply0":"“Ask me properly, and I'll show what I can,” Taffy says. “Don't take my only copy away.” Taffy wipes flour from the counter. “I sell sweets. I do not sell guesses.”","label1":"Ask if she suspects the Witch.","reply1":"“Suspicion isn't proof,” Taffy replies. “And I have had quite enough of people deciding the answer before they ask me.” “I want the truth out,” Taffy says. “I also want my neighbors safe.”"},"A3-10":{"speaker":"Gretel","setup":"Gretel lines up her dates and steps back. “Now tell me which part you think is weakest,” she says. “Not because I want to lose an argument. I want this answer to last.”","label0":"Point to the newest copy.","reply0":"“Good. We should find out where it came from before believing it twice.”","label1":"Say her calendar is beyond question.","reply1":"“Nothing should be beyond a fair question,” Gretel says. “Not even my calendar.”"},"HUB-03":{"speaker":"Pip","setup":"Pip has brought home a gingerbread figure shaped like himself. “I think the baker was being kind,” he says. “Though the nose is considerably larger than mine.”","label0":"Ask if he will eat it.","reply0":"“Only after I draw it for the journal. Future historians must know the truth about my nose.”","label1":"Ask whether the sweet shops will recover.","reply1":"“I hope so,” Pip says. “But people need the real records first, not a cheerful sign saying everything is fine.”"},"A4-01":{"speaker":"Archivist Tern","setup":"Tern points at the three doors. “The front door has rules,” he says. “The side door has rules. The third door is mostly a staircase, which seems to have escaped everyone’s attention.”","label0":"Ask which door he recommends.","reply0":"“The one that gives you the records you are allowed to see without stealing another person's privacy.”","label1":"Ask whether he ever breaks the rules.","reply1":"Tern adjusts his glasses. “I have made choices I wish I could explain better. You may ask me about them.”"},"A4-04":{"speaker":"Archive Assistant","setup":"The assistant rubs ink from their knuckle. “Someone left a false document on my desk,” they say. “I was there. That's all the court seems to need.”","label0":"Ask what they actually handled.","reply0":"“The catalog cart. The string on its handles. I never held the missing page.”","label1":"Promise to prove them innocent.","reply1":"“Please promise only to investigate carefully,” the assistant says. “I'd rather have the truth than a promise you can't keep.”"},"A4-05":{"speaker":"Archivist Tern","setup":"Tern looks at the folded order and says nothing for a moment. “A secret can feel like a wall that keeps danger outside,” he says. “I did not notice how easily it could trap people inside.”","label0":"Ask what he wants to put right.","reply0":"“The source of this document. My part in keeping it quiet. Then the people it affected should decide what to do next.”","label1":"Ask if he expects forgiveness.","reply1":"“I hope for a chance to help,” Tern says. “Forgiveness belongs to the people I hurt.”"},"A4-08":{"speaker":"Mistress Quill","setup":"Quill sets Nella's file on the table and keeps her hands in her lap. “There are things I meant to tell you when they were safe,” she says. “I kept moving the word safe farther away.”","label0":"Ask her to explain what she hid.","reply0":"Quill nods. “I'll tell you what I know, and when I knew it. You can be angry with me and still keep investigating.” Quill watches the rain. “Sometimes I hide a page because I fear what it will do. That fear can still hurt people.”","label1":"Tell her you're disappointed.","reply1":"“You have reason to be,” she says. “I won't make you comfort me about a mistake that was mine.” “I am responsible for what I kept quiet,” Quill says. “I can start by telling the truth.”"},"HUB-04":{"speaker":"Pip","setup":"Pip takes an unfinished book from the restoration table. “The empty space isn't frightening because there's nothing there,” he says. “It's frightening because someone might decide what goes there without asking.”","label0":"Tell him you miss the simpler mysteries.","reply0":"“So do I,” Pip admits. “My favorite mystery used to be who ate the biscuits.”","label1":"Ask if he's ready for court.","reply1":"“Not really,” Pip says. “But I have clean pages and two pencils. That will have to do.”"},"A5-01":{"speaker":"Mistress Quill","setup":"Quill stands in the side aisle where only you can hear her. “They will ask for a simple villain,” she warns. “Do not give them one unless the evidence truly does.”","label0":"Ask what to do if you make a mistake.","reply0":"“Correct it where people heard it. Quiet regret cannot repair a public accusation.”","label1":"Ask if the truth will be enough.","reply1":"“Truth is a beginning,” Quill says. “People still have to decide how to act on it.”"},"A5-02":{"speaker":"The Wolf","setup":"The Wolf watches the witness chair. “If I speak, people will call me brave. If I don't, some will call me guilty. Neither name tells them what happened.”","label0":"Tell him he may refuse.","reply0":"“Thank you. Then whatever I say will be mine.” The Wolf rests one paw on the floor. “A fair hearing should not depend on my being charming.”","label1":"Ask if a written account would help.","reply1":"“Perhaps,” he says. “Let me read it first and decide what may be shared.” “I can answer for what I did,” the Wolf says. “I cannot answer for every wolf in every story.”"},"A5-06":{"speaker":"Pip","setup":"Pip holds two chalks over the accusation board. “I could write a name in red,” he whispers. “Very dramatic. But then I would have to explain why it belongs there.”","label0":"Ask him to wait for evidence.","reply0":"“An excellent, terribly unexciting idea,” Pip says. “I approve.”","label1":"Ask if the crowd will be angry.","reply1":"“Possibly,” Pip says. “Crowds are often impatient with unanswered questions. We must not let impatience become proof.”"},"A5-07":{"speaker":"Archivist Tern","setup":"Tern turns his chair so he can hear the correction. “You may be embarrassed,” he says. “People may still be angry. The record should be accurate anyway.”","label0":"Ask whether errors can ever be erased.","reply0":"“The claim should be withdrawn,” he says, “but the record of the correction must remain.” Quill folds the first draft. “An apology is not magic. We still have work to do.”","label1":"Ask if this will ruin the trial.","reply1":"“No. It may be the first thing that makes the trial fair.” “You can correct a mistake in public,” Quill says, “without asking anyone to clap for you.”"},"HUB-05":{"speaker":"Mistress Quill","setup":"Quill opens the door to a tired witness who has come to sit in silence. “A court's verdict is not an ending for every person in the room,” she tells you quietly.","label0":"Ask how to help without questions.","reply0":"“Offer a chair. Offer water. Ask before asking for a story.”","label1":"Ask if there are still dangers ahead.","reply1":"“Yes,” she replies. “But tonight, nobody here must perform bravery for us.”"},"A6-02":{"speaker":"Elowen","setup":"Elowen kneels beside a vegetable bed at the edge of the unmapped settlement. “We have harvests here,” she says. “Birthdays. Arguments about fences. Being left out of a book did not end our lives.”","label0":"Ask what she needs from the visitors.","reply0":"“To be seen without being owned. That's harder than it sounds.”","label1":"Ask if she wants her story restored.","reply1":"“Maybe part of it. Maybe none. Ask me about the page itself when I've had time to think.”"},"A6-03":{"speaker":"The Ink Thief","setup":"The creature flickers from fox-shadow to feather and back. “People find one shape easier to blame,” it says. “If I have three, perhaps they'll argue about which one did it.”","label0":"Ask what it chose on its own.","reply0":"“More than I want to admit,” the thief says. “And less than my maker would have liked.” The Ink Thief watches its shadow move. “Even I am not sure which shape feels most like me.”","label1":"Ask what it fears.","reply1":"For a moment the fox shape goes still. “A future where I can only obey.” It looks away. “Saying no once does not undo what I did before.”"},"A6-06":{"speaker":"Rumpelstiltskin","setup":"Rumpelstiltskin spins one length of thread and stops the wheel. “A bargain,” he says, “is a story with a price written on the back.”","label0":"Ask to read the back.","reply0":"“A clever traveler,” he says, handing over the page. “Better than a clever signature.”","label1":"Tell him you don't trust contracts.","reply1":"“Then inspect the words,” he replies. “Not every agreement is a trap, but the trap is rarely printed in large letters.”"},"A6-07":{"speaker":"The Margin King","setup":"The King leads you past a mural of a burned village. “I watched good people lose everything,” he says. “I swore no one would ever face such a choice again.”","label0":"Ask whose choices he took away.","reply0":"He looks away from the mural. “The ones I feared would cause harm.”","label1":"Tell him fear does not grant ownership.","reply1":"“I once thought it did,” he says. “I am not certain how to build safety without it.”"},"A6-09":{"speaker":"The Ink Thief","setup":"The thief looks at the page it refused to destroy. “There was no line in the orders about stopping,” it says. “I stopped anyway.”","label0":"Ask if it will help repair the harm.","reply0":"“I can try,” it replies. “But I don't expect the people I frightened to call me a friend.” “An offered hand is not a bargain I must accept,” the Ink Thief says softly.","label1":"Tell it one good choice settles the past.","reply1":"“No,” it says. “I wish it did. I think I have more to do.” “I know a promise is only the beginning,” it says."},"HUB-06":{"speaker":"Pip","setup":"Pip runs his pencil along a page that is still missing its ending. “I wanted to mend everything the first time we met,” he says. “Now I think I want to learn when not to touch it.”","label0":"Ask if he regrets becoming a Keeper.","reply0":"“Not for a moment. Though I would appreciate fewer enormous machines trying to eat maps.”","label1":"Ask what hope looks like now.","reply1":"“A person who gets to choose,” he says. “And maybe a book that has room for another answer.”"},"A7-01":{"speaker":"Mistress Quill","setup":"Quill unfolds the bell diagram and turns it so everyone can see. “People call it powerful,” she says. “But a warning does not make decisions for the people who hear it.”","label0":"Ask why it cannot save everyone alone.","reply0":"“Because saving a person is not the same as ordering their future,” she answers.","label1":"Ask what the bell is for.","reply1":"“To tell us someone needs help. Then we must listen.”"},"A7-03":{"speaker":"Red Riding Hood","setup":"Red looks around the coalition table. “I don't want a plan that makes the forest safe only if the palace wins,” she says. “And I don't want the palace to fail for the sake of my woods.”","label0":"Ask how to keep everyone heard.","reply0":"“Let each place name its own danger. Then build a plan that has room for those answers.” Red taps the map. “If every plan needs every person, it is not much of a plan.”","label1":"Ask Red to lead the whole table.","reply1":"She shakes her head. “I can speak for my road. Not for every kingdom.” Jack raises his hand. “Can my job involve a rope?” Bram sighs. “Of course it can.”"},"A7-04":{"speaker":"Bram","setup":"Bram rests his huge hand beside the bridge design. “A road that only works for the strongest traveler isn't much of a road,” he says.","label0":"Ask what he wants from Jack.","reply0":"“Honesty about the risks. And a second person checking the knots.”","label1":"Ask whether a slow plan can still win.","reply1":"“Slow enough to be checked,” Bram says. “Fast enough to help. That's what we're trying to build.”"},"A7-05":{"speaker":"Cinderella","setup":"Cinderella lays out her proposed council plan. “I lived too long inside rules people called kindness,” she says. “Let's not build another rule that takes away someone else's voice.”","label0":"Ask who should decide about the palace.","reply0":"“The people who live and work there. The servants too. Especially the servants.”","label1":"Ask whether she misses simpler days.","reply1":"She laughs softly. “I miss a ballroom where the music stops when the musicians are tired.”"},"A7-06":{"speaker":"The Wolf","setup":"The Wolf looks at the blank witness card. “If I stand up for another accused person,” he says, “I do it because the evidence deserves a hearing. Not to make anyone like me.”","label0":"Ask whether he will travel with you.","reply0":"“If the plan respects the people it claims to protect, I may.”","label1":"Ask whether he trusts the court now.","reply1":"“I trust the people willing to correct it,” he answers. “Institutions have to keep earning that trust.”"},"HUB-07":{"speaker":"Pip","setup":"Pip sits beside a half-empty plate of biscuits. “Tomorrow may be a very large day,” he says. “I think I would like this ordinary minute to last a little longer.”","label0":"Sit quietly with him.","reply0":"The lamp flickers. Pip leans against your sleeve and lets the silence be comfortable.","label1":"Ask what he'll do when it's over.","reply1":"“Put the map away,” he says. “Have breakfast. Then probably find a new problem to worry about.”"},"A8-01":{"speaker":"Red Riding Hood","setup":"Red checks the caravan lanterns, then glances toward the tower. “I was told for years that courage meant facing a wolf,” she says. “Turns out sometimes it means walking beside one.”","label0":"Ask whether she is afraid.","reply0":"“Yes,” Red says. “I'm coming anyway. That's my decision, not an order.”","label1":"Tell her she has already helped enough.","reply1":"“Maybe,” she replies. “I still want to help now.”"},"A8-04":{"speaker":"The Margin King","setup":"The King places the master seal between you. “You could force a gentle ending,” he says. “No more arguments. No more wrong turns. No more pain caused by choosing badly.”","label0":"Ask who would get to refuse.","reply0":"He says nothing. The empty answer tells you more than the seal. The King hears the voices around him. For once, he has no ready answer.","label1":"Ask if he could live by someone else's chosen ending.","reply1":"The King lowers his hand. “I have never considered that I might be asking everyone else to do precisely that.” You let the silence stand. No one here needs you to speak for them."},"HUB-08":{"speaker":"Pip","setup":"Pip holds the Living Atlas open while Quill fetches a clean ribbon. “We finished one story,” he says. “It feels enormous. And somehow the book still has pages.”","label0":"Ask whether the next telling replaces this one.","reply0":"“Never,” Pip says. “This story happened. Another might happen beside it.”","label1":"Ask if he will come on another adventure.","reply1":"“You couldn't stop me,” he replies. “Although you may need to explain the same rules to me again. I forget things when excited.”"},"A1-R01":{"speaker":"Eira Glass","setup":"Eira watches the wrong-window chime fade. “You look as if you've heard that sound before,” she says. “I haven't. Tell me what you notice, not what you think I ought to remember.”","label0":"Explain only the sound you heard.","reply0":"“Good,” she says. “Then we can compare it with this window. This is our road, not somebody else's memory.”","label1":"Tell her you've solved this mystery before.","reply1":"“Perhaps,” Eira says. “But you haven't solved mine. Start with the glass.”"},"A1-R06":{"speaker":"Aster","setup":"Aster watches the stopped footprint. “I want you to know which way I ran,” he says. “I don't want you to tell the whole village where I was hiding.”","label0":"Offer to keep the location sealed.","reply0":"“Then I'll show you the path,” Aster says. “Only the part you need to follow.”","label1":"Ask for public testimony anyway.","reply1":"“Not today,” he says. “The path is still there. You can examine it without using my name.”"}};
const endings={"END-E01":"The next morning, the village bellkeeper asks for a volunteer to check the clapper. A carpenter raises her hand. Then a baker offers to carry warnings to the next road. No crown appoints either one. They write the duties down where everyone can see them. Pip adds a note to your journal: A warning is only useful if someone listens—and if anyone may ask who rang it.\n\nBy supper, the bell has a proper schedule and three volunteers. Pip says this is his favorite kind of magic: the sort people can count on.","END-E02":"Elowen reads the restored page aloud once, slowly. Halfway through, she stops and changes a word. The magic does not resist her. The line waits, as all honest promises should wait, until she is ready. Quill folds the page's old wrapper and gives it back to her. It is not a trophy for the bookshop. It is a story whose owner has chosen to carry it home.\n\nElowen places a finger beneath the final line. It is her ending now. That matters more than how brightly the ink shines.","END-E03":"At the first gathering after the danger, the Forest, Cloudreach, and Palace representatives disagree about what to do with a damaged bridge. It is a surprisingly cheerful argument. Bram draws a safer support, Nell checks the hours of the workers, and Red asks how the messenger will pass at night. The kingdoms have not become the same place. They have learned to make room for one another.\n\nNo one agrees on the meeting snacks. Everyone agrees the roads should remain open. Quill calls this a very promising start.","END-E04":"Two travelers arrive at the same crossroads carrying different maps. Both routes have been checked, and both are open. The travelers choose different paths, then wave goodbye with no fear that one must be the wrong life. In the Atlas, Pip draws the fork with two lamps instead of one. Their flames burn at the same height. Neither has to put the other out.\n\nAt the crossroads, neither traveler has to call the other wrong. Two lanterns burn, and both paths lead somewhere worth seeing.","END-E05":"The clerk reads the correction aloud at the market so nobody must search a locked library to find it. He names what the original paper claimed, what the evidence now proves, and where the protected records may be checked. Some listeners are furious. Others are relieved. Nobody is required to pretend the harm never happened. That afternoon, a child brings the clerk another old notice and asks whether it, too, should be examined.\n\nA little girl in the market asks the clerk to read the new record twice. He does. Truth should not be hidden behind a locked door.","END-E06":"The Wolf returns to the forest at his own pace. A village child notices him near the bridge, opens her mouth to shout, then looks at the fresh warning board. It speaks of false prints, safe lantern routes, and who to ask for help. The Wolf watches her read. He does not come closer. He does not have to. For once, the road gives him enough room simply to walk home.\n\nThe Wolf waits until the child has finished asking questions. Then he answers as himself, not as a monster from an old tale.","END-E07":"Bram turns the last page of his repaired calendar and finds a space where the lost day belongs. He writes its date in careful ink. Jack carries baskets across the tested bridge, and the baker brings an extra loaf to the people who helped repair it. Nobody pretends the old damage was harmless. They have simply made tomorrow a day people can rely on again.\n\nJack suggests declaring the repaired date a holiday. Bram says the calendar is not a party invitation. Jack begins planning anyway.","END-E08":"Midnight comes. Cinderella hears the first note of the final waltz and looks toward the musicians. One of them lowers her bow and asks for a rest. This time the room waits. Nell opens the servants' doors, the prince helps stack chairs, and the guests choose whether to stay for tea. Cinderella laughs when the clock finishes striking. At last it is only a clock.\n\nWhen the clock strikes twelve, nobody freezes. Cinderella laughs, takes off her shoes, and dances because she wants to.","END-E09":"At the first damaged crossing, the Ink Thief arrives in the shape of a small bird. Nobody mistakes its appearance for an apology. It shows the repair plan, helps replace the broken lantern glass, and leaves a clear record of what it did. Some villagers watch with crossed arms. Others hand over a tool. The thief works until the path is safe, then asks which harm it should address next.\n\nThe Ink Thief sets down its first repaired page. It is a small start. It does not ask anyone to call it a hero.","END-E10":"In a distant village, a teacher sets three copies of the corrected history on three separate shelves. One stays in the school, one travels to the next town, and one is kept by the families whose names it concerns. Each copy says where it came from and who may request a correction. If one shelf burns or one ruler changes a line, the others will still remember what happened.\n\nThe teacher invites children to compare the copies. “They should agree,” she says, “but you have every right to check.”","END-E11":"The new council holds its first meeting in a room with windows on both sides. A road worker disagrees with a royal clerk, and nobody calls for guards. They check the map together. The minutes record the disagreement, the evidence they considered, and when the decision will be reviewed. Outside, Pip puts the old throne's cushion on a bench where tired travelers may sit. He finds it much more comfortable there.\n\nThe council ends on time, to everyone’s surprise. Its first decision is to keep the doors open for people who still have questions.","END-E12":"When the last visitor leaves the survivor road, its keepers close the gate for the evening. They do not lock away the future. They are choosing a quiet night. A child hangs a lantern beside the latch, where a traveler may request entry tomorrow. The key stays with the community. Beyond the hill, the thirteenth bell rings once—not to give an order, but to say that someone has the chance to listen.\n\nAt dusk, the travelers return with different stories to tell. The road does not choose a favorite. Neither do its keepers."};
const echoes={"HUB-01":[{"from":"A1-W03","reply":0,"text":"Pip recalls the Wolf asking to be judged by his paws, not his reputation. He has written that sentence at the top of the new page."},{"from":"A1-W03","reply":1,"text":"Pip has written that anger and honesty can live in the same person. He leaves space below the sentence for the evidence still to come."}],"A2-05":[{"from":"A1-W03","reply":0,"text":"The Wolf notices that you once asked for something you could verify. It does not settle everything, but he stays long enough to listen."},{"from":"A1-W03","reply":1,"text":"The Wolf remembers your question about his anger. “I meant what I said,” he tells you. “I can be angry and still tell the truth.”"}],"A2-11":[{"from":"A2-02","reply":0,"text":"Pip double-checks his new stitches, remembering how frightened he was when an unfamiliar mark first appeared."},{"from":"A2-02","reply":1,"text":"Pip has kept the measured thread sample from the map. He shows it to you before drawing any conclusion about the Keeper."}],"A3-10":[{"from":"A3-03","reply":0,"text":"Gretel points back to the original recipe calendar you asked about. The earlier dates still support her comparison."},{"from":"A3-03","reply":1,"text":"Gretel remembers that you tried to hand the investigation to Hansel. She makes room for his observations without surrendering her own work."}],"HUB-04":[{"from":"A4-08","reply":0,"text":"Quill sees the note you made about her hidden file. “If you ask me again,” she says, “I will answer with what I actually know.”"},{"from":"A4-08","reply":1,"text":"Quill does not ask you to excuse her secrecy. She brings another chair and allows you to keep your disappointment."}],"A5-07":[{"from":"A5-01","reply":0,"text":"Quill's earlier warning returns to you: a public mistake needs a public correction, not a private wish that it would disappear."},{"from":"A5-01","reply":1,"text":"You remember Quill's careful answer: truth begins the work, but people still must decide how to act."}],"A7-03":[{"from":"A6-02","reply":0,"text":"You remember Elowen's garden outside the official maps. The coalition cannot treat a living community as an empty space."},{"from":"A6-02","reply":1,"text":"Elowen's question about restoring her story returns. The council must ask about each page instead of treating every repair as automatic."},{"from":"A1-W01","reply":0,"text":"Red remembers that you asked why her woods mattered. “Then help me make a plan people can live with,” she says."},{"from":"A1-W01","reply":1,"text":"Red remembers your question about the Wolf. She asks the council to use the actual evidence, not an old story about him."}],"HUB-07":[{"from":"A7-06","reply":0,"text":"The Wolf has not forgotten that you asked whether he would travel with you. Tonight, he has chosen his own place in the caravan."},{"from":"A7-06","reply":1,"text":"The Wolf watches the plan board, remembering your question about the court. “Make sure people can still correct it,” he tells you."},{"from":"A7-05","reply":0,"text":"Cinderella has written a reminder beside the palace plan: the servants must have their own vote."},{"from":"A7-05","reply":1,"text":"Cinderella laughs softly as she packs. “When this is done, I hope the musicians finally get to go home on time.”"}],"A8-04":[{"from":"A6-07","reply":0,"text":"You remember asking the King whose choices he took. The master seal in front of you looks less like safety and more like another stolen voice."},{"from":"A6-07","reply":1,"text":"The King has no answer for the idea that fear does not grant ownership. You do not need to invent one for him."}],"HUB-08":[{"from":"HUB-07","reply":0,"text":"Pip remembers the quiet minute you shared before the tower. He decides to leave one page of the Atlas blank for resting."},{"from":"HUB-07","reply":1,"text":"Pip has added “Breakfast” to his list of things worth saving. He shows you proudly, as if the word were a magical artifact."}]};
function callback(id,game){
  const choices=game.depthChoices||{};
  for(const e of echoes[id]||[]) if(choices[e.from]===e.reply)return e.text;
  return '';
}
g.OUATDepth={textures,dialogues,endings,callback};
})(window);
